---
import Doc from "../layouts/Doc.astro";

// The public form of SECURITY.md, plus how to report abuse on a pipe and what happens to it.
// When SECURITY.md changes, this page changes in the same pull request.
const UPDATED = "November 5, 2026";
const REPO = "https://github.com/handlemotion/wurmhol";
const REPORT = `${REPO}/security/advisories/new`;
const POLICY = `${REPO}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`;
const SELF_HOSTING = "/docs/self-hosting";

const title = "Security and abuse: Wurmhol";

const description =
  "How to report a vulnerability in Wurmhol, or abuse or phishing on a pipe at *.wurmhol.dev, and what happens next.";

const scope = [
  {
    what: "Pipe access",
    detail:
      "Only signed-in members of the org that owns a pipe may reach it. Anything that lets a signed-out viewer, a member of another org, or a removed member load a pipe's pages, or that forges or replays the one-time code or the __Host-wurmhol cookie.",
  },
  {
    what: "Isolation",
    detail:
      "A pipe's JavaScript must not read or set cookies of the app or another pipe, or act as the signed-in user on wurmhol.com.",
  },
  {
    what: "Tokens",
    detail:
      "Tokens are stored hashed and scoped to one org. Anything that reveals a token, lets one start pipes for another org, or keeps working after revocation.",
  },
  {
    what: "The relay",
    detail:
      "Request smuggling between streams, reading another pipe's traffic, or using the relay as an open proxy.",
  },
  {
    what: "Accounts and orgs",
    detail:
      "Sessions, passkeys and sign-in with Google or GitHub, and any way to read or change another org's data.",
  },
];

const takedown = [
  {
    what: "End the pipe",
    detail:
      "Its address stops answering. Pipe ids are random and never reused, so the same address does not come back.",
  },
  {
    what: "Revoke the token",
    detail: "The token that opened the pipe stops working, which ends every other pipe it opened.",
  },
  {
    what: "Block the org",
    detail:
      "For deliberate abuse, we block the org behind it: its other pipes end, and none of its tokens can start another.",
  },
  {
    what: "Reply",
    detail: "We tell you what we found and what we did, on the same report.",
  },
];
---

<Doc
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading="Security and abuse"
  lead="How to report a vulnerability in Wurmhol, or a pipe that hosts phishing, malware or other abuse, and what happens after you do."
  updated={UPDATED}
>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="contact">
    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <div class="body prose">
      <p>
        Report both vulnerabilities and abuse privately through GitHub: open <a href={REPORT}
          >Report a vulnerability</a
        > on the repository's Security tab. Only the Wurmhol maintainers see the report. A GitHub account is needed to send one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please don't open a public issue, pull request or discussion for either: a public report of
        a vulnerability puts people at risk before it's fixed.
      </p>
      <p class="contact">
        <a href={REPORT}>github.com/handlemotion/wurmhol/security/advisories/new</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="vulnerability">
    <h2 id="vulnerability">Report a vulnerability</h2>
    <div class="body prose">
      <p>
        Wurmhol relays traffic between a developer's local server and the people they share it
        with, so a security bug can expose someone's unreleased app or let a stranger in. We take
        reports seriously and thank everyone who sends one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Include what you can: the affected component and commit, steps to reproduce or a proof of
        concept, and the impact you expect. We aim to acknowledge a report within 3 business days,
        and to agree a disclosure date with you once a fix is ready. We credit reporters in the
        advisory unless you ask us not to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Test only against your own <a href={SELF_HOSTING}>self-hosted instance</a> or the local
        stack (<code>pnpm stack:serve</code>). Do not access other people's pipes or data, degrade
        hosted Wurmhol, or run automated scans against it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="scope">
    <h2 id="scope">In scope</h2>
    <div class="body prose">
      <p>The code in the repository, especially:</p>
      <dl class="rows">
        {
          scope.map((row) => (
            <div class="row">
              <dt>{row.what}</dt>
              <dd>
                <p>{row.detail}</p>
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <p>
        Out of scope: vulnerabilities in Cloudflare or Stripe themselves (report those to the
        vendor), what a member's own dev server does to that member, findings that need a
        compromised device, missing hardening headers without a demonstrated impact, and denial of
        service by volume.
      </p>
      <p>
        The relay can technically read the traffic it carries; that is stated plainly in the <a
          href="/privacy">privacy page</a
        >, and request bodies are never logged or stored.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="abuse">
    <h2 id="abuse">Report abuse</h2>
    <div class="body prose">
      <p>
        Every pipe on hosted Wurmhol has its own address under <code>wurmhol.dev</code>, such as
        <code>k3x9p2m7qa.wurmhol.dev</code>. Only signed-in members of the org that owns a pipe can
        open its pages; everyone else gets a sign-in page from Wurmhol, never the pipe's content.
      </p>
      <p>
        If a pipe hosts phishing, malware or anything else that harms people, or a link to one
        reached you, report it as above. Give the full pipe address, what you saw and when, and
        where the link came from. A screenshot helps. Don't send credentials you may have entered;
        change them instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        A pipe on a domain other than <code>wurmhol.dev</code> runs on someone's own Wurmhol
        install, not ours. Report it to whoever runs that domain.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="takedown">
    <h2 id="takedown">What we do</h2>
    <div class="body prose">
      <p>When a report holds up, we:</p>
      <dl class="rows">
        {
          takedown.map((row) => (
            <div class="row">
              <dt>{row.what}</dt>
              <dd>
                <p>{row.detail}</p>
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="versions">
    <h2 id="versions">Supported versions</h2>
    <div class="body prose">
      <p>
        Wurmhol has no tagged releases yet. Security fixes land on <code>main</code> and ship from
        there; hosted Wurmhol runs <code>main</code>, and the CLI is published from it. The policy
        this page is built from is <a href={POLICY}>SECURITY.md</a> in the repository, and its
        machine-readable form is <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Doc>

<style>
  .contact {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
